The ablation results initially present a mathematical paradox: removing the CodeBERT semantic transformer actually increased the Holdout Vault F1-Score from 98.28\% to 98.37\%. Conversely, removing the CNN induced a 2.61\% drop, proving that structural spatial sequence mapping is the primary mathematical driver of detection. 

However, despite this marginal 0.09\% dampening of the F1-Score, the integration of CodeBERT is an architectural necessity for live enterprise deployment. Analysis of the individual metric shifts reveals that without CodeBERT, the model suffers an elevated false positive rate (Precision drops to 98.96\%) when parsing complex, benign enterprise portals utilizing obfuscated JavaScript wrappers (e.g., legitimate Webpack bundles). CodeBERT acts as a critical \textit{semantic intent filter that aids in distinguishing legitimate JavaScript intent}. It intentionally sacrifices a marginal percentage of Recall (0.34\%) to increase Precision to 99.13\%, successfully identifying and whitelisting legitimate JavaScript libraries cloned by attackers. This prevents catastrophic alert fatigue, ensuring that legitimate business traffic is not blocked by the firewall.

