\subsection{Computational Performance and Deployment Viability}
To validate the ensemble's viability across diverse deployment environments, inference latency and peak memory utilization were profiled on both an edge-device proxy (Apple M-Series CPU) and an enterprise threat analytics server (NVIDIA RTX 5060 Ti GPU, AMD Ryzen 9 9900X). As detailed in Table \ref{tab:hardware}, the decoupled architecture eliminates the need for massive GNN graph-loading operations. 

On the edge-computing profile, the ensemble requires a remarkably lightweight peak memory footprint of just 75 MB, with an average end-to-end inference latency of 1.3 seconds (1295 ms) per URL. To ensure true edge-deployment viability, this research proposes a two-stage cascade architecture where the lightweight DOM parser (CNN1D) and heuristics serve as the first stage, handling 95\% of incoming traffic in under 46 ms. The computationally heavy CodeBERT model is activated only as a secondary semantic filter when confidence drops below a defined threshold.

Conversely, on the enterprise server profile leveraging CUDA acceleration, the Tri-Modal Ensemble executes with an average end-to-end inference latency of just 97.42 ms per URL and a peak memory utilization of 1.77 GB (1770.97 MB). This proves that OmniPhish can concurrently process real-time enterprise network traffic at scale without introducing perceptible latency bottlenecks.

